<BaseLayout
  title="The Frame Loop Is a Boundary | Steve Weiler"
  description="How FocalDesk keeps its Wayland compositor responsive by moving variable-latency work behind explicit, recoverable service boundaries."
>
  <main class="article-page">
    <article>
      <header class="article-hero">
        <a class="article-back" href="/engineering/"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> Engineering</a>
        <p class="eyebrow">Architecture · FocalDesk</p>
        <h1>The frame loop is a boundary.</h1>
        <p class="article-lede">
          Building a Wayland desktop forced me to decide what absolutely has to
          live inside the compositor—and what must be allowed to fail somewhere
          else. That boundary became the organizing principle for FocalDesk.
        </p>
        <div class="article-byline">
          <span>By Steve Weiler</span>
          <time datetime="2026-08-02">August 2, 2026</time>
          <span>7 minute read</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <figure class="article-hero-image">
        <img src="/images/focaldesk/desktop.png" alt="The FocalDesk desktop session with its top bar, side rail, and application windows" />
        <figcaption>FocalDesk running as a complete desktop session, not just a compositor demonstration.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="article-body">
        <p class="article-opening">
          A compositor sits at the center of a Wayland session. It receives input,
          decides where surfaces belong, coordinates frames, and presents pixels to
          displays. That centrality creates a dangerous temptation: when the desktop
          needs one more capability, the compositor is the easiest place to put it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Application launching? Add it to the compositor. Notifications? The
          compositor already draws the shell. Power controls, settings, screen
          capture, dialogs, model requests? They all need to interact with the
          session, so pulling them inward can feel efficient.
        </p>

        <p>
          It is efficient—until one slow operation becomes a dropped frame, one
          blocked request freezes input, or one recoverable service bug takes down
          the entire desktop. FocalDesk began as an experiment in building a Rust
          compositor with Smithay. It became a deeper exercise in deciding which
          responsibilities deserve to share a failure domain.
        </p>

        <aside class="article-callout">
          <span>The core rule</span>
          <p>If work can wait on a process, device, network, model, or person, it should not own the frame loop.</p>
        </aside>

        <h2 id="compositor-is-not-desktop">The compositor is critical. It is not the whole desktop.</h2>

        <p>
          The frame loop has a simple but unforgiving job: remain responsive. Its
          responsibilities include surfaces, focus, workspaces, input routing,
          output state, shell geometry, rendering, and frame coordination. These
          concerns share tight timing and state relationships, so keeping them
          together is useful.
        </p>

        <p>
          Most desktop features have a different operational shape. Launching an
          application may touch the filesystem and parse desktop entries. A power
          action crosses a privileged system boundary. A notification can arrive
          while its client is disappearing. An AI request can take seconds and may
          need explicit approval before it does anything at all.
        </p>

        <p>
          Those operations do not become predictable merely because they are called
          from predictable code. The architecture has to acknowledge their latency
          and failure modes. In FocalDesk, supporting capabilities run as separate
          user services and communicate through typed IPC. The compositor remains
          the authority for session state, but it does not have to perform every
          piece of work itself.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="article-diagram">
        <img src="/images/focaldesk/architecture-overview-v2.svg" alt="FocalDesk architecture showing applications, the compositor and renderer, hardware backends, and out-of-process services connected through typed IPC" />
        <figcaption>The boundary follows timing and failure behavior, not an arbitrary split between “core” and “features.”</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="article-body">
        <h2 id="contracts">A process boundary is only as good as its contract.</h2>

        <p>
          Splitting a system into processes does not automatically make it robust.
          It exchanges local complexity for distributed-systems complexity: message
          versioning, timeouts, partial failure, startup order, stale state, service
          discovery, and observability all become explicit engineering work.
        </p>

        <p>
          That trade is worthwhile when the boundary reflects a real difference in
          ownership or failure behavior. FocalDesk uses request, response, and event
          contracts so callers can distinguish “accepted,” “completed,” “denied,”
          and “unavailable” instead of assuming that sending a message means the job
          is done. Services can restart independently; callers must be prepared for
          them not to exist yet—or to disappear mid-operation.
        </p>

        <div class="article-principles" aria-label="Service boundary principles">
          <section>
            <span>01</span>
            <h3>State has an owner</h3>
            <p>Each fact has one authoritative home. Other processes receive snapshots or events, not shared mutable state.</p>
          </section>
          <section>
            <span>02</span>
            <h3>Failure is a response</h3>
            <p>Unavailable and denied are normal outcomes that the interface must represent, log, and recover from.</p>
          </section>
          <section>
            <span>03</span>
            <h3>Latency is visible</h3>
            <p>A request that can take time is modeled as asynchronous work, never disguised as a quick local call.</p>
          </section>
        </div>

        <h2 id="permissions">Permissions belong in the interaction, not in a policy file.</h2>

        <p>
          AI integration made the architecture’s consequences especially clear.
          Generating text is one category of operation. Opening an application,
          reading a file, changing a setting, or controlling the session is another.
          A desktop should not collapse those categories into one blanket grant.
        </p>

        <p>
          FocalDesk routes action requests through a native permission path. The
          request stops, the desktop shows what is being requested, and a person can
          approve or deny it before execution continues. The permission decision is
          part of the protocol, not a decorative prompt layered over an action that
          has already begun.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="article-inline-image">
        <img src="/images/focaldesk/ai-permission.png" loading="lazy" alt="FocalDesk AI Console displaying a native permission request before an action proceeds" />
        <figcaption>A model request pauses at a desktop-owned permission boundary.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="article-body">
        <h2 id="technology">Technology choices follow the boundary.</h2>

        <p>
          Rust, Smithay, and OpenGL ES are important parts of FocalDesk, but none of
          them is the architecture by itself. I chose Smithay because it provides
          Rust-native Wayland building blocks without prescribing the desktop
          around them. Rust’s ownership and concurrency rules help enforce
          invariants in session-critical code. OpenGL ES supplies the rendering
          capabilities this compositor needs without taking on Vulkan’s explicit
          resource and synchronization surface.
        </p>

        <p>
          Each choice is intentionally bounded. Rust can prevent broad classes of
          memory errors, but it cannot decide who owns a piece of distributed state.
          IPC can isolate a crash, but it cannot invent a recovery strategy. A fast
          renderer cannot compensate for blocking work in the input path. The design
          still has to make the system’s promises clear.
        </p>

        <h2 id="result">What the boundary buys—and what it costs.</h2>

        <p>
          The immediate benefit is a smaller critical core. Supporting services can
          evolve, restart, and fail without automatically ending the graphical
          session. The same seams also make the desktop easier to inspect: service
          health, messages, permissions, and state transitions are things the system
          can expose instead of hiding them inside one process.
        </p>

        <p>
          The cost is rigor. There are more lifecycle states to test, more contracts
          to document, and more degraded modes to design. Startup and shutdown need
          orchestration. Logs need enough context to connect events across process
          boundaries. Compatibility changes must be deliberate.
        </p>

        <p>
          For FocalDesk, that is the right complexity to own. A desktop session is a
          long-running system that mediates between hardware, applications, services,
          and a person. Pretending those pieces fail together cleanly would make the
          implementation simpler only on paper.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          The goal is not the fewest processes. It is the smallest failure domain
          that still preserves coherent ownership.
        </blockquote>

        <h2 id="next">A working rule for systems beyond the desktop.</h2>

        <p>
          The frame loop is specific to a compositor, but the design question is
          general: what part of your system carries the promise users notice first?
          It may be an event loop, an API request path, a control plane, or a storage
          transaction. Protect that path from work with unrelated latency and failure
          behavior. Give the displaced work a real contract, a clear owner, and an
          honest degraded mode.
        </p>

        <p>
          FocalDesk is still alpha software. Some services and first-party
          applications are prototypes, and several APIs are evolving. But the system
          is far enough along to validate the architectural bet: a desktop can feel
          like one cohesive product without being one indivisible process.
        </p>
      </div>

